{# The topics picker. A native <dialog>, opened by a local signal rather than a server round
   trip -- "toggling element visibility" is exactly what the Tao of Datastar says signals are
   for, and the server has no interest in whether a panel is open.

   Ticking boxes changes `$topics` (no underscore, so it is uploaded) but commits nothing: the
   working set only changes on Apply, as in the panel version. Clear is pure client state. #}
{# Escape closes it, and closing it discards -- same path as the Close button. A NON-modal dialog
   (`open`, not `showModal()`) gets no Escape handling from the browser, so it is wired by hand; the
   guard on `$_topicsOpen` keeps the key free for anything else when the picker is shut. #}
<dialog class="topics-dialog" id="topics-dialog" data-attr:open="$_topicsOpen"
        data-on:keydown__window="evt.key === 'Escape' && $_topicsOpen
          && ($_topicsOpen = false, @get('{{ prefix }}/filter/topics-reset{{ variant_query }}'))">
  <h2>Topics</h2>
  <p>
    Pick any number — an auction matching <em>any</em> selected topic is included. Nothing
    changes until you press <strong>Apply</strong>, which replaces whatever is in the filter box.
  </p>
  {# Everything that can grow is inside ONE scroller, and the buttons are outside it. The whole card
     used to scroll, so with nineteen topics Apply / Clear / Close were sliced in half at the bottom
     edge -- the actions of a dialog should never be the thing you have to scroll to find. The list
     itself flows into as many columns as the card is wide enough for (see `.topic-list`), so on a
     desktop the nineteen fit without scrolling at all. #}
  <div class="topics-scroll">
    <div class="topic-list">
      {% for topic in topics %}
      <label class="checkbox">
        <input type="checkbox" data-bind:topics.{{ topic.slug }}
               data-on:change="@get('{{ prefix }}/filter/preview-topics{{ variant_query }}')" />
        {{ topic.name }}
      </label>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>
    <div id="topics-status" class="filter-status"></div>
    {% if topics | selectattr('description') | list %}
    <details data-preserve-attr="open">
      <summary>What the topics mean</summary>
      <ul class="topic-legend">
        {% for topic in topics if topic.description %}
        <li><strong>{{ topic.name }}</strong> — {{ topic.description }}</li>
        {% endfor %}
      </ul>
    </details>
    {% endif %}
  </div>
  <div class="dialog-actions">
    <button type="button" class="button is-primary" data-on:click="$_topicsOpen = false; @post('{{ prefix }}/filter/apply-topics{{ variant_query }}')">Apply</button>
    {# `@setAll(false, {include: /^topics\./})`, NOT `$topics = {}`.

       The boxes are bound one signal at a time (`data-bind:topics.<slug>`), so `topics` is a
       NAMESPACE, not a value: assigning an empty object to it replaces the branch the bindings are
       watching instead of writing to the leaves, and every box stayed ticked. `@setAll` walks the
       signal tree and writes each matching leaf, which is what the checkboxes are actually bound to.

       The `@get` after it is not decoration: the status line under the list is server-rendered from
       the ticks, so without it Clear leaves "N topics selected" sitting under an empty list. #}
    <button type="button" class="light button is-light"
            data-on:click="@setAll(false, {include: /^topics\./}); @get('{{ prefix }}/filter/preview-topics{{ variant_query }}')">Clear</button>
    {# Close is CANCEL: it discards the ticks and puts them back to the filter in force (see
       `app.topics_reset`). The dialog's first line promises that nothing changes until Apply, and
       keeping an abandoned draft is the one way to break that promise without touching the filter --
       reopen and the picker is showing a selection the app is not using. #}
    <button type="button" class="light button is-light"
            data-on:click="$_topicsOpen = false; @get('{{ prefix }}/filter/topics-reset{{ variant_query }}')">Close</button>
  </div>
</dialog>
